me leads  into the second biggest problem that we  heard  time and time again was low viability  the lower the viability the  more and more dead cells we fought which  are not very useful to us at all but  these are not the cells that we want to  sequence  in fact most single cell sequencing  technologies  recommend that your sample be at least  75  viable the lower the viability  of the sample the more cleanup processes  you're going to have to do with your  cells  meaning that the whole process takes a  lot longer  again going back to issue number one  the third problem i've got on here is  sample size and availability  now particularly during this pandemic  time  although these this research we did  was conducted prior to the pandemic  sample availability was already an issue  this is dwindled more and more as we  speak  sample sizes are reducing as well  because  in a good way technologies now enable  surgeons and nurses and those involved  to take smaller and smaller biopsies  from our patients  in order to minimize their discomfort  but that means that by the time the  pathologist has looked at the samples  and they're available to the researcher  they've got very little material to work  with and then if of that very little  material the viability is low  you've got even less to work with  therefore it's incredibly important  that from that small tissue sample that  is incredibly precious have we  even managed to get hold of it that we  get the best information that we can  from that sample  in the bottom corner here i've already  iterated cell stress and transcription  changes  because this goes hand-in-hand with  everything  all the other challenges on here  and finally the last two on my slide  here  are poor reproducibility and automation  which again go hand in hand  the current processes are not automated  which means they are very user dependent  and we all know but between myself and  my colleagues no matter how hard we try  there will be inherent variability  and the more variability we introduce  into our results  the more we will end up doubting the  final results and  trying to decipher what is true and what  is not true  so these are the challenges that are  currently being faced with tissue  disassociation  and they really stem from  the workflow in which tissue  disassociation is typically conducted  so i've put together here a summary of  the typical manual disaggregation  process  the first of all the experiments begin  by taking your piece of tissue and the  most basic of equipment  a pair of scalpels or scissors and  tweezers  and you are to mince the tissue to as  fine as possible  one to two millimeter squared or a grain  of rice they say  as you can see from the image this is  quite easy to conduct  but quite rudimentary it can take about  five to twenty minutes depending on the  size of the tissue the toughness of the  tissue  it could even take longer then we scrape  up those  pieces of tissues and put them into our  enzyme cocktail  depending on the nature of your enzymes  this you may need to incubate your  sample at 37 degrees  or in a cold room or in an ice bucket  this process can take from 30 minutes to  overnight  and depending on whether you have a  rotating mechanism or not  that will constantly rotate the sample  you may have to go in yourself and  periodically shake or trickery  your sample to release the cells often  finally to separate out the remaining  clumps from your cell suspension  you use a cell strainer all of these  processes as you can see are subject to  temperature variations  time variation and user dependent  variabilities  just going to go over these in a little  bit more detail for example  at the first stage when we are cutting  the tissue by cutting into the tissue  rapidly to make them as small as  possible we're effectively slicing  through  many many cells rupturing them rendering  those cells useless for experiments  it is also difficult to regulate the  temperature during mincing quite often  this is conducted  inside a cell cultural tissue culture  hood of which temperature is  purely dependent on what the room  temperature is  and finally before we pick up our  samples and put them  into our tube of enzymes  we are bound to lose some material to  the list as to the dish as we will never  be  able to recover every single bit of that  sample  variability like i said is also caused  by  different procedures and different  people for instance i may find that  chopping the tissue slowly but gently is  better  yet a colleague of mine may say let's do  it as fast as possible  but they might be operating much harder  either way what  how are we to know which is the best  procedure and  how much impact these cells are having  not on  not just visually on your sample but  right down to the genomic level  finally i'd like to mention how we  noticed in that filter above  there's a lot of sample that was  remaining in the cell strainer  after the complete disassociation  process  that again is a subset of sample that we  will never  analyze or get the meaningful data from  we could choose to digest that even more  but chances are then that sample has  been stressed so much that how can we be  sure  that what we're seeing there is not an  artifact of the stress caused by the  amount of digestion put forward on that  sample  so in order to address these challenges  and questions  sativa have been working on a solution  in order to  optimize the cell viabilities and yields  and to be able to preserve that  transcriptome  and reflect the original state of that  tissue  we want to be able to take into  consideration  the variation in tissue composition  which is important for single cell  sequencing  maybe even incorporate the extracellular  matrix and look at heterogeneity in the  protocol  and finally hopefully detect those rare  cells which we're unable to do by bulk  sequencing  so due to restrictions that are  currently imposed it means that until  this product is fully launched  unfortunately i can't show you the  product nor can i give you the name of  the product  but i really hope to be able to do so in  the future  in the meantime however i'm going to try  to describe the product to you  to the best that i am allowed to do so  in the case of sativa's new solution we  will be placing the tissue  in a single-use disposable bag format  in this format the tissue will never get  be in contact with any mechanical  or sharp parts of the instrument  in fact the the tissue which you do not  need to mince  will be placed into the disassociation  chamber  which can be controlled to a temperature  of your desire  the mechanism will act upon your tissue  and little as five to ten minutes your  sample could be fully disassociated  and ready for further processing  downstream  so to put this into a little bit more  context this process is  gentle due to the lack of any sharp  edges  or anything touching the tissue and it  is also  quick meaning that cell viability and  yield are much  improved and i don't need to take my  word for it as in the coming few slides  i'll be showing you some of the data  the instrument is programmable and you  can  set the temperature anywhere between 0  degrees  and 40 degrees celsius meaning that  you are free to optimize this and choose  the temperature  best suited to your enzymes needs  you are able to put in the enzyme  cocktail of your choice  meaning that that will be one less  variable that  you as a researcher need to consider and  you can choose the enzyme cocktail that  you know to be perfect  for your tissue sample already the  limited amount of materials  and equipment that we have in this  process by eliminating things such as  disused  silver scissors scalpels we  are not likely to lose material  moreover and again i will go on to show  you  that with our system we have a complete  cell suspension  there's very little remaining on that  cell strainer  when you finish the process the fact  that this is  automated means that we can avoid user  variability  as day by day no matter who uses the  system  the output should be the same  provided that the sample is the same of  course if the sample has variability  in itself there's very little that we  can do  but most importantly this procedure  is rapid it is much quicker than the  current methods that i've described  as even in 30 minutes we're able to  disassociate  very tough tissues such as the mouse  lung tissue  so now as i've promised i'll go on to  show you some of that performance data  here i'm comparing tissues disassociated  using our automated system  versus the manual disaggregation that i  described a couple of slides earlier  first and foremost the table on your  left shows the  average sampled incubation times at 37  degrees celsius  this is to say that with our automated  samples  this is the time taken for the full  full tissue disaggregation as i  mentioned earlier  you can put in the whole sample no need  for pre-mincing  into the disassociator and this is the  length of time that we have optimized  for the full disassociation of the  given samples here the mouse lung kidney  liver and spleen  however with the manual i've only  recorded here the one constant  the time that we leave it in the  incubator for however the length of time  that it might have taken for the  preparation of these samples the manual  mincing of these samples into the small  grains  is of course variable and an additional  time that you need to add  onto the time indicated here all of  which of course are already longer  than the time taken for the automated  processing  next following tissue disaggregation  you can pass the cells through the cell  strainer to remove the  cell clumps however as you can see from  the automated disaggregation  from the lung kidney liver and spleen  very little if  any solid bits of tissue are left in the  strainer  meaning that a hundred percent of that  sample has flown through  and will be available for your analysis  however with the manual processing you  can see  much of that tissue still remains  meaning that those samples will  or that tissue will not be available for  analysis later in the in your process  following the tissue disaggregation and  cell straining  the cells will be centrifuged and the  red blood cell lysis  step continues continued on  following which we use an automated cell  counter  in order to count the cells and assess  the cell viability  what you can see instantly is of all  four tissues that we have  been working on that viability is higher  in fact in the case of the mouse lung 50  percent higher than the  manual processes that are currently  being used and in all cases you can see  how good  the variability is between  the samples processed with the automated  compared to the manual process  by having over 75 viability  or cases hundred percent of times would  remove the need for post-processing like  the removal of dead cells and there  would also of course be less debris in  these samples  reducing the potential of logging  blocking the fluidic channels of the  single cell sequencing  machinery following on from cell  viability  we're able to count the cells i've  represented the cell yields  by counting the number of viable cells  per milligram  of input tissue to ensure that all  experiments are equally comparable  you can see again that in all cases of  tissues  we have much higher yields of  cells remaining following the automated  disassociation compared to the manual  disassociation  in fact tens to hundreds of thousands  more cells  per milligram can be analyzed with our  system  this is fantastic particularly if you're  wanting to look for those rare cells  and achieve the concentrations required  for your single cell sequencing  experiments  meaning that you can be sure or you are  more likely to find  information that has not been discovered  before  finally i'd like to draw your attention  to a  different experiments here showing how  well  we can disassociate tissues that are  small in size typical biopsy size  tissues  as little as 25 milligrams to large  pieces of tissue  such as a one gram of mouse liver  or one whole kidney bearing in mind that  none of these tissues have been  pre-minced  you can see here that the viabilities of  these samples remain incredibly high  despite the size of the tissue and we  also have very comparable yields  despite the size of the tissue  this is fantastic in giving us  confidence that we are not losing any  material  and that we are successfully able to  disassociate  even when sample availability might be  compromised but  reasons beyond our control  so by way of summary i've brought us  back to this slide of challenges  but the automated solution that sativa  proposed and are hoping to launch in the  near future  is addressing the fact that the current  procedure  takes a long time we've our new solution  is actually much quicker allowing you to  just disassociate tissues  in less than five minutes  we've also addressed low viability as  you seen from my data  viabilities much improved from our  process often hitting  close to 100 viable cells and we've also  been able to disassociate  very small pieces of tissue just as  successfully  as larger pieces of tissue the fact that  we are able to do this in a more gentle  manner  in a quicker manner means that we were  already  quite confident that cell stress and  transcriptome changes can be minimized  but in order to look into this in a  little bit more detail  we are currently working on analyzing  the single cell profile of these cells  and we hope to be able to share that  data with you very soon  finally by introducing automation to the  tissue desegregation process  we are able to reduce variability  and the poor reproducity problems that  have been faced so far  allowing for more streamlined  experiments in the future  so that's all